- What?
- He was from the last season.

1819
01:06:18,969 --> 01:06:20,169
That's right.

1820
01:06:20,169 --> 01:06:24,009
(The members enjoyed the jar barbecue.)

1821
01:06:24,409 --> 01:06:26,379
Did they consult him?

1822
01:06:27,279 --> 01:06:29,479
No wonder the way it hung inside the jar...

1823
01:06:29,749 --> 01:06:31,049
looked so familiar.

1824
01:06:32,119 --> 01:06:34,089
Honestly, when he said he did a pre-roast,

1825
01:06:34,089 --> 01:06:36,719
- I almost bought into it.
- I know. There were two jars.

1826
01:06:36,719 --> 01:06:38,029
I know.

1827
01:06:38,029 --> 01:06:39,929
It sounded like professional knowledge.

1828
01:06:39,929 --> 01:06:41,559
He actually tested it out.

1829
01:06:41,729 --> 01:06:42,729
(After 15 minutes at 150°C, the roasted fruit is done.)

1830
01:06:42,729 --> 01:06:43,769
(The production crew is excited.)

1831
01:06:44,499 --> 01:06:46,369
(They look decent.)

1832
01:06:48,039 --> 01:06:50,539
He actually tried it all himself.

1833
01:06:50,539 --> 01:06:52,069
He even tested different temperatures.

1834
01:06:52,069 --> 01:06:55,179
(The jar legend is savouring it with caution.)

1835
01:06:55,579 --> 01:06:56,939
This one has no flavour.

1836
01:06:57,109 --> 01:06:58,609
We shouldn't roast this one.

1837
01:06:59,779 --> 01:07:01,419
It's better not to roast this one, either.

1838
01:07:01,879 --> 01:07:03,149
(The jar master's harsh criticism)

1839
01:07:03,149 --> 01:07:04,749
He tested it all.

1840
01:07:04,819 --> 01:07:05,919
It should be cooked longer.

1841
01:07:05,919 --> 01:07:07,689
The natural tartness is gone.

1842
01:07:07,859 --> 01:07:08,889
I don't like this.

1843
01:07:08,889 --> 01:07:10,359
(It's not so good.)

1844
01:07:11,129 --> 01:07:13,259
But the one we ate was very sweet.

1845
01:07:13,529 --> 01:07:15,929
(Coming to save the jar-roasted fruit in crisis)

1846
01:07:15,929 --> 01:07:18,799
- I guess a chef is stepping in.
- Right.

1847
01:07:19,199 --> 01:07:20,199
Who is it?

1848
01:07:20,199 --> 01:07:22,739
(Chef Park Joon Woo)

1849
01:07:22,739 --> 01:07:24,069
Chef Park Joon Woo.

1850
01:07:24,069 --> 01:07:27,679
(Runner-up of "MasterChef Korea Season 1")

1851
01:07:27,939 --> 01:07:29,039
(Dessert cafe owner-chef and fruit dessert specialist)

1852
01:07:29,039 --> 01:07:31,379
How did he make it? It was really good.

1853
01:07:31,609 --> 01:07:33,379
We want to make a sweet dessert...

1854
01:07:33,379 --> 01:07:36,279
by roasting fruit in a jar. Is there a way to pull that off?

1855
01:07:36,279 --> 01:07:37,749
Simply roasting fruit alone...

1856
01:07:37,749 --> 01:07:39,519
won't make the fruit itself much sweeter.

1857
01:07:39,689 --> 01:07:41,919
You just need to add sugar or syrup.

1858
01:07:42,159 --> 01:07:43,219
(Easy)

1859
01:07:43,219 --> 01:07:44,959
I can't believe this.

1860
01:07:44,959 --> 01:07:47,259
(The real reason the fruit was sweet: Sugar and syrup)

1861
01:07:47,259 --> 01:07:48,729
They are frauds.

1862
01:07:48,729 --> 01:07:49,759
I know.

1863
01:07:50,499 --> 01:07:51,699
I got goosebumps.

1864
01:07:52,569 --> 01:07:54,999
We need a marinade to make it special.

1865
01:07:55,339 --> 01:07:56,999
Let's make a syrup. It's really easy.

1866
01:07:58,039 --> 01:07:59,509
- This is vanilla bean.
- So they marinated it.

1867
01:07:59,509 --> 01:08:02,239
That's why it tasted like canned fruit earlier.

1868
01:08:02,239 --> 01:08:03,239
(Adding alcohol for aroma and sweetness)

1869
01:08:03,239 --> 01:08:05,579
A lot of sugar goes into the marinade.

1870
01:08:06,279 --> 01:08:07,879
Right, it tasted like apple jam.

1871
01:08:07,879 --> 01:08:08,919
Exactly.

1872
01:08:08,919 --> 01:08:10,519
I knew it.

1873
01:08:10,519 --> 01:08:11,549
(Then what is the original identity of this place?)

1874
01:08:11,549 --> 01:08:12,749
What is this place?

1875
01:08:13,719 --> 01:08:16,619
(A hanok cafe located in Insa-dong)

1876
01:08:17,229 --> 01:08:18,559
(An empty front yard)

1877
01:08:18,559 --> 01:08:19,859
- Right.
- I see.

1878
01:08:21,159 --> 01:08:23,929
So they did construction work to prevent sparks.

1879
01:08:23,929 --> 01:08:25,029
I find this even more surprising.

1880
01:08:25,029 --> 01:08:26,629
- My goodness.
- This is a lot of work.

1881
01:08:26,629 --> 01:08:29,369
- They even did that?
- They worked so hard.

1882
01:08:30,609 --> 01:08:32,069
It's unbelievable.

1883
01:08:32,069 --> 01:08:33,069
(Getting earthenware jars from Yangpyeong)

1884
01:08:33,069 --> 01:08:34,439
- They got them from Yangpyeong.
- Yangpyeong?

1885
01:08:34,579 --> 01:08:36,679
Transporting something so fragile like that...

1886
01:08:36,809 --> 01:08:39,709
- They put in a lot of effort.
- It's amazing.

1887
01:08:39,849 --> 01:08:41,719
The jars looked used, though.

1888
01:08:41,719 --> 01:08:42,919
Right.

1889
01:08:42,979 --> 01:08:44,749
It looked quite old.

1890
01:08:44,749 --> 01:08:46,819
(And today's B-Side)

1891
01:08:46,819 --> 01:08:48,259
- B-Side.
- Where was the B-Side?

1892
01:08:48,259 --> 01:08:52,229
(It was right before your eyes all along.)

1893
01:08:52,429 --> 01:08:54,029
(Did you take a good look at your surroundings?)

1894
01:08:54,029 --> 01:08:55,099
What was there?

1895
01:08:55,099 --> 01:08:57,529
(You didn't even need to look.)

1896
01:08:57,999 --> 01:08:59,899
- "You didn't even need to look."
- Why?

1897
01:08:59,899 --> 01:09:02,269
(Because it was in your hands the entire time!)

1898
01:09:02,599 --> 01:09:04,709
In our hands? When?

1899
01:09:05,609 --> 01:09:07,239
(The thing you had in your hands at the first place)

1900
01:09:07,239 --> 01:09:09,039
- Spoons?
- The spoons with 92?

1901
01:09:09,479 --> 01:09:12,009
(The six spoons)

1902
01:09:12,009 --> 01:09:13,709
(What do they all have in common?)

1903
01:09:13,709 --> 01:09:15,479
(They were born in 1992.)

1904
01:09:15,649 --> 01:09:17,289
My goodness. Nice.

1905
01:09:17,819 --> 01:09:19,749
- Well done.
- We're improving.

1906
01:09:19,819 --> 01:09:21,459
(What everyone held in their hands at the second spot)

1907
01:09:21,459 --> 01:09:22,759
H.O.T.

1908
01:09:23,689 --> 01:09:24,929
Candy!

1909
01:09:24,929 --> 01:09:27,129
